# 🔍 NLP Basics: Tokenization, Embeddings, Transformers

Natural Language Processing (NLP) is the field of enabling machines to understand and process human language. This article explains **Tokenization**, **Embeddings**, and **Transformers**, the foundational building blocks of modern NLP systems.

---

## 1. 🧩 Tokenization: Breaking Down Language

**Definition:**  
Tokenization is the process of splitting text into smaller units (tokens). Tokens can be **words**, **subwords**, or **characters**, depending on the method used.

### Types of Tokenization

| Type           | Example Input              | Tokens                      |
|----------------|----------------------------|-----------------------------|
| Word-based     | "ChatGPT is awesome!"      | ["ChatGPT", "is", "awesome!"] |
| Character-based| "ChatGPT"                  | ["C", "h", "a", "t", "G", "P", "T"] |
| Subword-based  | "unhappiness"              | ["un", "happi", "ness"]     |

### ⚙ Common Libraries
- **NLTK** (Natural Language Toolkit)
- **spaCy**
- **HuggingFace Tokenizers** (supports BPE, WordPiece, etc.)

### Visual Example
```
Sentence: "Transformers revolutionized NLP."

[Tokenization]
--> ["Transformers", "revolution", "##ized", "NLP", "."]
```

_Subword tokenization_ handles out-of-vocabulary words by breaking them into known chunks.

---

## 2. 🧠 Embeddings: Representing Text Numerically

**Definition:**  
Embeddings map tokens to **dense vectors** in a high-dimensional space. These vectors capture semantic meaning.

### Why Not One-Hot Encoding?
One-hot vectors are sparse and don't reflect meaning. E.g., "dog" and "cat" are unrelated in one-hot form, but similar in embedding space.

### Popular Embeddings

| Model     | Description                                     | Output Dimension |
|-----------|-------------------------------------------------|------------------|
| Word2Vec  | Predicts word from context (CBOW/Skip-gram)     | 100–300          |
| GloVe     | Captures global word co-occurrence statistics   | 50–300           |
| FastText  | Uses subword information                        | 100–300          |
| BERT/ELMo | Contextual embeddings based on full sentence    | 768–1024         |

### Visual Example
```
Embedding Space (2D Projection):

      dog ●       cat ●       lion ●
                  car ○       apple ○
```
Vectors for similar concepts (animals) are closer.

